
// draft
substrings(iterable: Int[]) -> Int[][]
	note "Every contiguous non-empty run of the input, shortest first, and within one length in order of where it starts."
	# Every output element is the slice of the input from some position up to but not including a
	# later position.
	// reaching for: for every s in out, there exist integer positions i and j with 0 <= i < j <=
	// len(iterable) such that s == iterable[i .. j]. The notation quantifies only over list
	// elements, never over a range of integer positions, and the end position len(iterable) is not
	// the position of any element.
	# Every slice of the input with at least one element is in the output.
	// reaching for: for all integer positions i and j with 0 <= i < j <= len(iterable), iterable[i
	// .. j] in out. This needs a quantifier over a range of integers, which the notation lacks.
	# The output has one element for each pair of start and end positions: twice the output's length
	# is the input's length times one more than the input's length.
	len(out) * 2 == len(iterable) * (len(iterable) + 1)
	# No output element is longer than an output element that comes after it.
	all a in out: all b in out where a before b in out: len(a) <= len(b)
	# The output elements of any one length are the input's slices of that length, in order of their
	# starting position.
	// reaching for: for every length k from 1 to len(iterable), the sub-list of out whose elements
	// have length k equals the list [iterable[0 .. k], iterable[1 .. 1 + k], ...,
	// iterable[len(iterable) - k .. len(iterable)]]. This needs a filter producing a sub-list of
	// out and a quantifier or list built over integer start positions, and the notation has
	// neither.
	// example wanted: the empty input, where there are no positions to start from
	// example wanted: a one-element input, where the only slice is the whole input
	// example wanted: [0, 1, 2] from the docstring, where the order by length and then by start
	// position decides the layout
	// example wanted: [0, 0], where two slices are equal in value but start at different positions,
	// so the output keeps both rather than deduplicating
